course: medicine
lesson: 72
title: Bike helmets and roads
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ryan in Newcastle sets off with his helmet hanging off the handlebars, «Howay, helmets are mid, man» («mid» is young slang for rubbish); but when you come off a bike your head can hit the kerb hard, and the foam in a helmet crushes and spreads the hit so less of it reaches your brain.
explain: A helmet only works on your head, level, about two fingers above your eyebrows, buckled with room for about one finger under the strap; pushed back like a halo it leaves the forehead bare, and unbuckled it flies off before you land.
explain: Helmet foam is made to crush once, so after a crash you get a new one even if it looks fine; a bad head injury can change a life for good, and some children are killed on bikes every year on UK roads.
explain: Roads need more than balance: lights after dark, ears free of music, never riding out between parked cars, never riding up beside a lorry, and brakes checked before every ride; Bikeability training teaches road riding on real roads.
explain: A car at 30 mph needs about 23 m to stop, and a lorry driver sitting high up may not see a child next to the cab at all, so the rider who stays seen and stays back is the one who gets home.
fact: In a fall a helmet's foam crushes and spreads the force so less reaches the skull and brain.
fact: In the UK wearing a cycle helmet isn't the law, but the Highway Code (the UK's book of road rules, rule 59) says cyclists should wear one that fits and is fastened properly.
fact: A helmet should sit level, about two fingers' width above the eyebrows, with room for about one finger under the buckled chin strap; pushed back, it leaves the forehead bare, and many falls land on the front of the head.
fact: An unbuckled helmet comes off in a crash before the head hits the ground.
fact: Helmet foam is built for one big hit; after a crash it may be damaged inside even if it looks fine, so it should be replaced.
fact: By law, a bike ridden at night in the UK needs a white front light, a red rear light and a red rear reflector.
fact: Drivers see a small rider in dark clothes very late, especially in rain and at dusk.
fact: Hearing warns you about cars behind you and around corners, so earbuds take away that warning.
fact: The Highway Code gives a stopping distance at 30 mph of about 23 m: about 9 m thinking plus 14 m braking.
fact: Riding out between parked vehicles means drivers can't see you until the last moment.
fact: Lorry drivers have big blind spots down the left side, and a cyclist beside a lorry turning left can be crushed; stay behind lorries.
fact: Bikeability training has 3 levels, from control in the playground to quiet roads to busier roads, and is often done in Year 5 or 6.
fact: Feet can't stop a bike at speed; brakes, tyres and chain should be checked before every ride, and a bike with a broken brake isn't ridden.
fact: «Howay» is Geordie for come on; «dreich» is Scots for grey and wet; «takkies» is South African for trainers.
exercise: How does a bike helmet protect your head? | The foam crushes and spreads the hit | It bounces you back onto the bike | It stops you from ever falling off in the first place | It keeps your head warm in the wind
exercise: Ryan hangs his helmet on the handlebars. Where does it protect him? | Nowhere; it only works on his head | On the handlebars, it still counts | Only if he crashes into the handlebars | Everywhere, as long as he owns one
exercise: Is a cycle helmet the law for UK cyclists? | No, but the Highway Code says you should wear one | Yes, the police fine you on the spot | No, and nobody advises it | Yes, but only on Sundays
exercise: Noah wears his helmet pushed back like a halo. What's the problem? | His forehead is bare, and many falls are forwards | Halos make helmets heavier | The helmet will get too hot | Nothing, any angle protects you
exercise: How high above the eyebrows should a helmet sit? | About two fingers | About a hand and a half | Right on top of the eyebrows | About ten fingers
exercise: Chloe leaves her chin strap flapping. What happens in a crash? | The helmet comes off before her head lands | The flapping strap catches in the front wheel | Nothing, the helmet stays on anyway if it's tight | The loose strap makes the helmet even stronger
exercise: Arjun's helmet has a small crack after a crash. What now? | Get a new helmet | Tape it up and keep riding | Use it until it splits in two | Turn it round the other way
exercise: Callum rides home at December dusk with no lights. What does the law need? | White front, red rear light, red rear reflector | Just a bell and a basket | Only a reflector on the pedals | Nothing, if he knows the road
exercise: Lily rides with music loud and doesn't hear a van beep. What's the rule? | Ears free when you ride | Turn it up so you hear it over traffic | Use just one louder earbud | Music only on main roads
exercise: Dev rides out between two parked vans. Why is that dangerous? | Drivers can't see him until the last moment | Vans always roll backwards | Cars can stop in about a second | Parked vans can set off by themselves without warning
exercise: About how far does a car at 30 mph need to stop? | About 23 m | About 2 m | About 230 m | About 1 m
exercise: Grace can ride fast in the park. What should she do before riding to school on the road? | Do Bikeability training | Nothing, it's the same thing | Ride faster to beat the cars | Ride only on the pavement edge
exercise: Finn stops beside a lorry's front left wheel. What's the rule? | Stay behind lorries, never beside on the left | Wave at the driver so he sees you | Go first when the lights change so he sees you | Hold onto the lorry for balance
exercise: Sipho's bike has a broken back brake. What's the rule? | Broken brakes, no ride | Drag your shoes to stop | Only ride down hills | Use the front brake extra hard
exercise: A helmet costs about £20 and a bike about £200. How many helmets for the price of one bike? | 10 | 20 | 180 | 2
exercise: How many levels does Bikeability have? | 3 | 1 | 5 | 10
test: «It's not even the law, so I'm not bothering». What's the smart answer? | It protects your head; that's the point | If it's not the law, then it doesn't matter at all | Helmets only work if it's the law | Only grown-ups need protecting
test: Noah: «it's giving angel, no cap». What's the finger check? | 2 fingers above eyebrows, 1 under the strap | 1 finger above the ears and 2 fingers on the nose | 5 fingers above the eyebrows, no strap | Thumbs up and ride off
test: Chloe: «the click pinches, innit». What should she do? | Ask an adult to adjust it, and click it every time | Leave it undone, it's fine | Cut the strap off | Wear it backwards
test: Arjun: «it's only a little crack, still good, innit?» What would happen in his next crash? | The damaged foam might not protect him | It would protect him twice as well as before | Cracks make helmets stronger, like old bones | Nothing, little cracks don't matter at all
test: Callum: «it's pure dreich, but I know the road». Does knowing the road help drivers see him? | No, drivers need lights to see him | Yes, drivers know he knows the road | Yes, if he rides very fast | No, but rain makes riders glow
test: «crap, he beeped twice and she didn't even turn round». What took away Lily's warning? | Loud music in both ears | A sore neck from riding | The van's quiet engine | The helmet covering her ears
test: Dev: «cars stop, don't they?» How far would a car at 30 mph travel before stopping? | About 23 m | About 2 m | It stops instantly | About half a metre
test: Finn: «he'll see me, I'm right here». Why might the lorry driver not see him? | Lorries have big blind spots down the left side | Lorry drivers keep their eyes shut | Bikes are invisible in daylight | Lorry windows are painted black
test: Sipho: «Lekker, no stress, I'll just drag my takkies» downhill to a junction. What would happen? | His shoes would slide and he couldn't stop | His shoes would stop him easily, like brakes do | The hill would slow him down | Junctions always have soft walls
test: In a cartoon a kid crashes his bike, bounces off the road and rides away laughing. What's real? | Heads hit kerbs hard, and injuries can last for life | Kids always bounce off roads unhurt | Bikes can't crash on real roads | Only grown-ups get hurt on bikes
test: Grace: «I'll just ride on the road, it's the same thing». What's the difference? | Traffic needs extra skills the park doesn't teach | Roads are smoother, so it's easier | Cars always have to give way to children on bikes | There is no difference at all
exam: Pursuant to Rule 59 of the Highway Code, identify the recommendation in respect of cycle helmets. | A helmet which fits and is correctly fastened should be worn | Helmets are prohibited on public roads | Helmets are required only for adults | Helmets are to be carried on the handlebars
exam: It is given that a helmet has sustained an impact in a collision. State the appropriate course of action. | The helmet is to be replaced | The helmet is to be repaired with adhesive tape | The helmet is to be worn reversed | No action is required if it appears intact
exam: Determine the approximate total stopping distance of a car travelling at thirty miles per hour, as stated in the Highway Code. | Twenty-three metres | Two metres | Two hundred and thirty metres | Nine metres
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hazard posed to a cyclist positioned alongside a lorry turning left? | The cyclist may be within the driver's blind spot and be struck | The cyclist is invariably visible to the driver | The lorry is unable to turn left | The cyclist is protected by the lorry's mirrors
